---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/saniger-place.json";
---
<Base
  title="Saniger is a place"
  eyebrow={`${d.deeds.length + d.medieval.length} records · from 1272 to 1833 · found ${d.searched}`}
  dek="This archive has spent a fortnight treating Saniger as a surname that happens to cluster near Berkeley. It is the other way round: Saniger is a hamlet in Berkeley parish, and the name comes off the land.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Every page here has said the same thing in the same order — a family called
    Saniger, found at Berkeley, then at Dursley and Cam. <strong>The order is wrong.</strong> The
    National Archives' catalogue describes men who are "<em>of Saniger, Berkeley</em>", and farms
    and lands lying "<em>in Saniger and Hamfallow</em>". Saniger is a place, and the surname is
    taken from it.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>Which explains the thing this archive kept noticing and could not account for.</strong>
      It has repeatedly recorded that the name "concentrates in the Vale of Berkeley", that the
      spellings are chaotic, and that "of Berkeley" looked like a label somebody applied loosely.
      A toponymic surname does exactly that: it is invented once, in one parish, and everyone who
      carries it is from there. <a href={u("/name")}>How surnames are coined →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The catalogue entries</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Two centuries of Sanigers at Saniger</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Reference</th><th class="m">Date</th><th>Entry</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.deeds.map(([ref, when, what]) => (
            <tr><td class="m mono">{ref}</td><td class="m">{when}</td><td class="small">{what}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">Almost all of these are <span class="mono">D2957/41</span>
    at <strong>Gloucestershire Archives</strong> — the Berkeley estate deeds. They are catalogue
    descriptions, not documents this archive has read.
    <span class="chip inf">catalogued, not yet seen</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Berkeley Castle Muniments</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The place is two hundred years older than the name</h2>

    <p class="lede">The Berkeley family kept its own archive, and it is catalogued. Nine of its deeds
    name people <em>of Saniger</em> between about 1272 and 1473 — and <strong>not one of them is
    called Saniger</strong>.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Reference</th><th class="m">Date</th><th>Who</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.medieval.map(([ref, when, who]) => (
            <tr><td class="m mono">{ref}</td><td class="m">{when}</td><td class="small">{who}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>Gamel, Waleys, Nel, Couthulf, Smyth, de Egton, Hevyner, Webbe.</strong> Eight surnames
      across two centuries, all of them living at Saniger and none of them named for it. The place is
      there before the family is — and <span class="mono">BCM/A/1/30/2</span> puts it beyond argument:
      "<em>Geoffrey Nel of Saniger <strong>in the parish of Berkeley</strong></em>".
      <br /><br />
      That is what settles the direction. A surname taken from a place can only be formed after the
      place has a name, and here the gap is documented: people are <em>of</em> Saniger under other
      names from before 1291, and the first man actually called Saniger appears in 1601.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Bigland, 1791</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">"Saniger, long held by an old Family of the same Name"</h2>

    <p class="lede">Ralph Bigland was Garter King of Arms and spent decades copying every monumental
    inscription in Gloucestershire. His account of Berkeley parish says the thing this archive worked
    out from deeds, in one line — and then gives the stones.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Inscription</th><th class="m">Date</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.bigland.monuments.map(([who, when, note]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{who}</strong></td><td class="m">{when}</td><td class="small">{note}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>This is the material Berkeley's parish transcript does not have.</strong> FreeREG's
      Berkeley St Mary stops in <strong>1677</strong>. Bigland's monuments run from 1692 to 1750,
      with names, relationships, exact dates and ages — a hundred and twenty years of the parish,
      copied by an antiquary and printed, sitting in a book on archive.org the whole time.
      <a href={u("/berkeley")}>The page that has been complaining about that gap →</a>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>And they were freeholders.</strong> {d.bigland.freeholder}</p>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.1rem">Bigland also lists their neighbours at Saniger, which
    tells you what kind of place it was — a hamlet of a dozen households, not an estate village:</p>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:.8rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>{d.bigland.neighbours.map(([who, when]) => (
          <tr><td class="small">{who}</td><td class="small m">{when}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The older name</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Before it was Saniger it was Swanhanger</h2>

    <p class="lede">{d.swanhanger.claim}</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>One deed, catalogued twice, settles it.</strong> {d.swanhanger.proof}
      <br /><br />
      {d.swanhanger.gloss} <span class="chip doc">documented</span>
    </div>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Spelling</th><th class="m">When</th><th>Where it is written</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.swanhanger.forms.map(([f, w, src]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{f}</strong></td><td class="m">{w}</td><td class="small">{src}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.swanhanger.people}</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>And they are all standing in the same field.</strong> {d.swanhanger.field}
    </div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">Swan-hanger is ordinary Old English: <em>hangra</em>, a
    wooded slope. The place is a wooded slope above the Severn where swans were. Everything else —
    the hamlet, the surname, the woman who carried it to Queensland — comes off that.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Gloucestershire Archives, D2957/41</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">A clothier in 1692, a gentleman in 1709</h2>

    <p class="lede">{d.estate.note}</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Reference</th><th class="m">Date</th><th>Named</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.estate.rows.map(([ref, when, who, what]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="mono small">{ref}</td>
              <td class="m">{when}</td>
              <td><strong>{who}</strong></td>
              <td class="small">{what}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">{d.estate.reading}
      <span class="chip doc">documented</span></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Bigland's Berkeley, hamlet four</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Who else was "of Saniger"</h2>

    <p class="lede">{d.hinton}</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">{d.neighbours.note}</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          {d.neighbours.rows.map(([who, what]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{who}</strong></td><td class="small">{what}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.1rem">{d.minchinhampton}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Probate, and one inquest</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Four records that name Sanigers outside a parish register</h2>

    <p>Probate is the record type that names children. This archive had never searched it for this
    surname. Four things came back, and one of them is not a will at all.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Reference</th><th class="m">Date</th><th>What it is</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.wills.map(([ref, when, what, why]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="mono small">{ref}</td><td class="m">{when}</td>
              <td><strong>{what}</strong></td><td class="small">{why}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>Elizabeth Saniger of Thornbury is the one to open.</strong> She died a widow in
      December 1833, in a parish that sits between Berkeley and Bristol and holds eight Saniger
      register entries. <a href={u("/people/hannah-saniger")}>Hannah Saniger</a> was married with
      children in Bristol by then. A widow's will names her children, and that is precisely the
      document this archive has never had. <a href={u("/errands")}>On the queue →</a>
      <span class="chip inf">worth opening; not yet opened</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">What it does to the oldest claim</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">A yeoman in 1601, and a gentleman clothier in 1692</h2>

    <p class="lede">The archive's deepest published person is a William Sanigar said to be born in
    1654. These entries reach fifty years behind him and describe a family already established:
    <strong>John Saniger of Berkeley, yeoman, in a release of 2 November 1601</strong>.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">And they name a man the registers have already produced.
    <a href={u("/berkeley")}>The Berkeley page</a> found three William Saingers baptised in ten
    years, every one the son of an Edward — and noted that one entry styled the father
    "<strong>Mr</strong> Edward", which in 1665 marks a gentleman rather than a labourer. The
    catalogue has him:</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Edward Saniger of Saniger, Berkeley</strong> — a <strong>clothier</strong> in 1692, a
      <strong>gent</strong> in 1709 and again in 1716, mortgaging and exchanging land with coopers
      of Berkeley and clothiers of Dursley.
      <br /><br />
      That is not proof that he is the Edward of the 1655 baptism; the dates would make them father
      and son at best. It is something better than this archive had yesterday: a documented family of
      the right name, in the right parish, at the right social level, in the trade
      <a href={u("/sanigers")}>the reconstitution found them in</a> — cloth.
      <span class="chip inf">the same family, almost certainly; the same man, not shown</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What this changes, and what it does not</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>"Of Berkeley" was never a loose label.</strong> It is
      where the name comes from. This archive should stop apologising for it and start saying it
      properly.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The cloth trade runs through the whole thing.</strong>
      A clothier at Saniger in 1692; weavers, tuckers and curriers at Cam and Dursley through the
      eighteenth century; and a family that leaves for Bristol when that industry collapses.
      <a href={u("/sanigers")}>The reconstitution →</a></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>There is a documentary spine at Gloucestershire
      Archives</strong> — <span class="mono">D2957/41</span> — that nobody has opened, covering
      exactly the years Berkeley's parish transcript does not.
      <a href={u("/errands")}>Now on the queue →</a></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The name has a meaning, and it is a landscape.</strong>
      Swanhanger — the wooded slope where the swans were, above the Severn. Every Saniger in this
      archive is named after a hillside. <span class="chip doc">documented from 1377</span></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>There is a will to open.</strong> Elizabeth Saniger,
      widow of Thornbury, proved 24 December 1833. It is digitised at Kew.</li>
      <li><strong>And it still does not reach Hannah.</strong> Nothing here connects the Saniger
      landholders of Berkeley to the woman who married James Wakefield and went to Queensland. That
      join remains where it was: in a Bristol chapel register, or in Berkeley's own book after 1677.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</Base>
